/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148486 #edb/ref 1061 632750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148486 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c0e51654-69d7-5097-b27f-71f3c28bce97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148486 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148487 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148487 #edb/ref 1061 632753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148487 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "99330381-c770-5a62-a3c7-a79ba13fcd0b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148487 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186148671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148488 #edb/ref 1061 632754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148488 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5102e680-a474-5349-a6c9-27bc67303b7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148488 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148670 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose Ethan and Ryan are baseball players. In the previous season, $24\\%$ of Ethan’s hits were home runs, while the decimal proportion of Ryan’s hits that were home runs was $0.3$.\n\nLet’s determine whether either Ethan or Ryan hit home runs on more than half of their total hits.\n\n- *For Ethan*, we compare his statistic to one-half written as a percentage, $50\\%$. Since\n$24\\% < 50\\%$,\nless than half of Ethan’s hits were home runs.\n- *For Ryan*, we compare his statistic to one-half written as a decimal, $0.5$. Since\n$0.3 < 0.5$,\nless than half of Ryan’s hits were home runs.\n\nTherefore, neither player hit home runs on more than half of their hits.\n\nNow, let’s see more concrete examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148670 #edb/ref 1065 14935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148670 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fbb4d977-7db5-579e-baf0-bb8e13b6c15a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148670 #edb/ref 1067 "Comparing Decimals and Percents in Context" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148671 #edb/ref 1064 "Now, suppose Carlos and Henry have the same number of trading cards. A total of $32 \\%$ of Carlos's cards are rare cards, while\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nof Henry's cards are rare cards.\n\nWho has more rare cards?\n\nTo answer this, we need to *compare the fraction and the percentage*:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad 32 \\%\n$$\n\nA common method is to convert the fraction to a percentage.\n\nFirst, we write\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nas an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} = \\dfrac{1 \\times 25}{4 \\times 25} = \\dfrac{25}{100}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{25}{100} = 25 \\%\n$$\n\nSince $25 \\% < 32 \\%$, we can conclude that Carlos has *more* rare cards than Carlos." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148671 #edb/ref 1065 14937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148671 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4be68676-8fcc-56ce-a8ef-96b2a954a03c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148671 #edb/ref 1067 "Comparing Fractions and Percents in Context" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148698 #edb/ref 1064 "When working with fractions, percents, and decimals, we often need to decide which number is greater or smaller. Since these numbers are written in different forms, it can be difficult to compare them directly.\n\nTo make comparisons easier, we can *convert them to a common form*.\n\nFor example, let's compare the percent $48\\%$ and the decimal $0.52$.\n\nOne way to do that is to convert the percent to a decimal. So, we divide the percent value by $100{:}$\n\n$$\n48 \\div 100 = 0.48\n$$\n\nThus, $48\\%$ is equivalent to $0.48$.\n\nNow, we can compare the two decimals:\n$0.48 < 0.52$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude that $48\\%$ is smaller than $0.52$.\n\nLet's see more examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148698 #edb/ref 1065 2078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148698 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e6681afa-a432-5074-a46c-204561f2cf65" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148698 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152124 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049429 #edb/ref 17592186045417